you weren't, and we knew you weren't. You just for whatever reason didn't have the power to completely change the game to your liking, only some parts of it.<br /> <br /> I think the apex for me was part 4 of the Giovanni adventure cycle where you suddenly had to play completely different, basically neonates, and the book lectured you on what an idiot you are to think you'd keep playing the same characters you were for the first three parts because playing characters who had over time earned becoming that powerful was for dumb dumbs who can't understand good gaming. I exaggerate, but not that much.<br /> <br /> Achilli may be a nice guy in person, but he has always had an adversarial attitude to his own audience, defends that attitude, hits a given game with a hammer from mechanics to setting until the end result reflects that attitude, and screams a vibe of failed novelist who is offended that you are getting your grubby nerd mits on his artistic masterpiece. Self awareness of that doesn't matter when he thinks he's justified anyway. And that level of puritanism (which again, he comes off as thinking is absolutely correct, and fuck, has said so online when talking about how um actually games should be narrow like Pendragon in what they let you do, in reference to Vampire 5th) helped V5 reach it's current &quot;shit, we're out of ideas, time to do everything we were previously calling stupid and impossible&quot; era.<br /> <br /> If he feels he didn't communicate certain things well in the books, it's hard for me to think he means that in any way other than the problem was more people should have agreed with what he was saying because of how right he is, not that there has ever been a problem with his ideas, his writing, or his developing, or his desire to have as restricted an audience as possible as long as they all agree with him.<br /> <br /> I say this by the way as someone that really heavily/seriously started playing VtM with Revised (and then went back to look at earlier books for being big into VtM at this point), and will always look back at revised with fondness for it being my gateway edition, as it were. I love that era. I love them books, warts and jank rules and all. But man, and lots of Revised fans will tell you this, there were a lot of times I loved it in spite of what the game was trying to do, not because of it, as it screamed into my ear like a petulant, snot nosed, crying, spoiled child &quot;NOOOO, YOU'RE DOING VAMPIRE WRONG! STOOOOOOP!&quot;<br /> <br /> V5 being so mechanically and narratively punitive to enforce how narrow and small it demands that it be, and a few moves in some of VtR definitely reflect a sometimes presence of some of the revised era writers and devs that seemed to have hated the game they were working on in that era, or at least that people had wide supportable options to play and experience it &quot;incorrectly&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>This is true. Back in the revised era it REALLY tried to continuously express the &quot;you're playing it wrong idea&quot;. I really enjoyed Revised, it's my favorite system and I still play it (I never saw the need to upgrade to V20 since the Kickstarter method was impossible to afford here in South America back then). It started with the novels killing Vitel, the writers tried to get rid of everything wacky from 1st and 2nd ed.<br /> <br /> I believe the fans' love ended with The Red Sign, I never got to be what we really expected from it. All of us wanted a Tremere/Order of Hermes conspiracy, specially after Blood Treachery came out and we got Giovanni/Technocracy. I admit it's a good book but not palatable for us back then. Lair of the Hidden was supposed to be the &quot;Guide to the Inconnu&quot; and also fell a little bit short, yet really enjoyable. I'm not even addressing Gehenna since it's useless and we all know it.<br /> <br /> On Justin I have huge respect for him, he was really welcoming as &quot;el_bastardo&quot; in the old forums. I really don't know the insides on how or why things happened but his books were he best (except NYbN, didn't like that one).<br /> <br /> - Saga","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1594-saga","name":"Saga","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-28T13:04:11-08:00","id":1557061,"title":"","content":"The Red Sign made.. choices. You could throw a rock and hit a revised era book that had highly varied reception from the audience, but anecdotally I knew but one person in my VtM gaming community of sorts (that was still quite big in those days) that liked that one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12141-markk","name":"MarkK","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-28T16:03:08-08:00","id":1557069,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1555226-bloodlines-2-and-the-current-state-of-paradox?p=1557026#post1557026\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br />